    "lawCode": "Amd §5, Judy L",
    "lawSection": "Judiciary Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6077A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the judiciary law, in relation to the conducting of\narbitration and mediation on Saturday and/or Sunday in certain cases\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo allow Sabbath observers to conduct binding arbitration and\nmediation on Saturdays or Sundays.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends section 5 of the Judiciary Law to allow arbitration\nor mediation proceedings be conducted on Saturdays or Sundays when all\nparties and the tribunal consent to such proceeding in writing.\n\nSection two is the enacting clause.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nArbitration and mediation are cost effective and efficient means to\nsettling disputes without intervention of courts. Alternative means of\nresolution of disputes is a positive step to achieve justice for\naggrieved parties in New York State. Unfortunately, the Judiciary Law\nand the cases decided therewith have held that arbitration proceedings\nare analogous to \"court\" proceedings under the law and, thereby,\npursuant to Judiciary Law, Section 5 - not permitted on Sunday or\nSaturday if any of the parties are a Sabbath observer, even if all\nparties consent to the arbitration.\n\nThis bill allows arbitration proceedings on weekends with consent of\nall parties and the arbitrator or tribunal conducting the arbitration.\nThis makes proceedings more equitable to parties by allowing such\nparties to conduct arbitration at more convenient times for their\nneeds and possibly prevent lost time from work or school.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nUndetermined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
